WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.062
We are now moving from reading data to changing data
This is

00:00:04.075 --> 00:00:09.088
where safe habits become especially important A
SELECT statement normally builds a

00:00:09.112 --> 00:00:14.475
result without modifying the stored rows INSERT
UPDATE and DELETE are different

00:00:14.488 --> 00:00:18.800
because they can change the database Before running
one of those statements

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:25.025
use a controlled process First preview the target
rows with SELECT If

00:00:25.038 --> 00:00:28.450
you plan to update a patient identified by an MRN
first run

00:00:28.475 --> 00:00:32.525
a SELECT using that same MRN in the WHERE clause
Confirm that

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:36.725
the row you expected is the row that appears Second
use a

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:42.700
stable identifier A name may be repeated misspelled
or changed A

00:00:42.712 --> 00:00:47.588
primary key or a unique MRN is usually safer Third
use a

00:00:47.612 --> 00:00:52.262
transaction when appropriate A transaction can let
you test a change and

00:00:52.275 --> 00:00:56.575
then roll it back instead of keeping it Fourth use
RETURNING when

00:00:56.588 --> 00:01:01.712
PostgreSQL supports the pattern RETURNING shows the
rows affected by the write

00:01:01.738 --> 00:01:07.475
operation Finally verify the result Do not assume
that success

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.175
means correctness The most important warning on this
slide is about WHERE

00:01:13.062 --> 00:01:16.175
An UPDATE or DELETE without a WHERE clause can
affect every row

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.388
in a table That is why previewing and verifying
should become normal

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.562
habits not optional extra steps
